 @Hardwareunboxed  I would be perfectly fine with 8GB GPUs in 2025 or even 2030 if they were $200 including inflation.

I quit caring about increased visual details in games 10+ years ago. Most modern games still look more than good enough for me even at their lowest details and I always reduce details well below any performance reasons to reduce or eliminate things that cause me unnecessary visual fatigue or irritation anyway.

For people like me who don't care about VRAM-eating details, 8GB will still be good enough for the foreseeable future. The only problem is the price of those 8GB cards, and perhaps their branding in the 60-tier instead of reviving the 50-tier.

 @tractor6619  Next to nobody owns a >240Hz display. This also holds true for most CS players, especially those looking to buy a 350$ GPU.
Also the upgrade from 240Hz to 360Hz only gets you an advantage of a 1,4ms better frametime. The human error while playing is so big you probably can't even reliably measure real world difference outside a reasonable margin of error...
The main reason to benchmark CS with cards in this performance segments are the percentile lows, as they will be a good bit lower and therefore have more impact.

